Battlecard — “What the fine print really says” (Years 1–2): 
- Nimbus: big Q4 headline discount tied to a 120‑hour “foundational” bundle that must cover onboarding in ~90 days — overruns billed T&M;  
- Nimbus: 120 hours in the draft has no measurable deliverables or acceptance criteria — scope ambiguity invites post‑signing compression;  
- Nimbus: implementation model allows partner handoffs / unnamed third‑party work — diffused accountability and likely pass‑through partner fees;  
- Nimbus: SLA relief is narrow (99.5% uptime trigger, 10 business‑day claim window, credits capped at 10% of month) — practically hard to collect meaningful remedy;  
- Nimbus: renewal language permits “up to 7%” increases in years 2–3 with no hard contractual cap — initial ACV savings can evaporate;  
- Nimbus: prepaid fees are non‑refundable on exit and termination requires notice — exiting mid‑term is costly and recovery is limited;  
- Nimbus: change‑orders/add‑ons are the obvious lever to claw back margin after signature;  
- Our side: fixed ACV, written SOW with hours/deliverables and acceptance criteria, in‑house delivery, transparent SLA credit math, and an explicit 2‑year price commitment — simple, accountable, no surprises.

Discovery questions reps should use (ask verbatim): 
- Exactly how will the 120 implementation hours be allocated by task and week?  
- Who will perform each implementation task — Nimbus employees or named third‑party partners? If partners, please name them and show partner rates.  
- What specific deliverables and measurable acceptance criteria are included in the foundational package?  
- What percentage of comparable deals exceed 120 hours and what is the average overrun in hours?  
- After 120 hours, what T&M rates apply and are there any caps on billed hours or daily limits?  
- Show the SLA credit calculation with a worked example (e.g., a 99.0% month) and state when credits are paid.  
- Confirm whether the 10‑business‑day claim window is the sole remedy and describe the exact claim process/required evidence.  
- Is “up to 7%” a contractual cap or discretionary language — if capped, point to the clause and formula; if discretionary, what usage thresholds prevent increases?  
- If we terminate in year 1 or 2, what notice, termination fees, and retained prepaid amounts apply, and what are data extraction/transition costs?  
- List all fees and partner/pass‑through charges excluded from the foundational package and provide historical change‑order size ranges.  
- How often has Nimbus actually held price flat in year 2 — provide counts/percentages and the commercial conditions that drove that outcome.  
- Who is the single accountable legal entity for delivery and dispute resolution (Nimbus or an implementation partner), and who signs the SOW?

Battlecard — Nimbus Q4 Fine‑Print Fear Check (Years 1–2):  
- Nimbus: steep Q4 headline discount tied to a thin “Standard Success” bundle (≈120 hrs) that must cover onboarding in ~90 days — overruns billed T&M;  
